#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "BASE_DOMAIN=nocturne.example.com bash <(curl -fsSL https://github.com/nightscout/nocturne/releases/latest/download/oracle-cloud-install.sh)"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "An <0>Oracle Cloud account</0>. Sign-up asks for a payment card to verify your identity. Always Free gives you 2 Ampere cores, 12 GB of memory and 200 GB of storage; this guide uses half the processor and memory and a quarter of the storage, so nothing is charged. Oracle reduced that allowance in June 2026, so older guides quoting 4 cores and 24 GB are out of date."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "A domain name. Nocturne gives every site its own subdomain, so the DNS provider must support wildcard records. If you do not have a domain, a free one from deSEC works well; see below."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "A decision about how to keep the server from being reclaimed as idle. It changes the command you run, so make it before you start."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Create an account at desec.io and confirm your email address."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Under <0>Domains</0>, add a dynDNS domain and pick a name. Your domain is then <1/>."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Under <0>Token management</0>, create a token and copy the long secret it shows you once — not the name you gave the token. You can delete the token afterwards."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Which one should you pick?"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Option 1: upgrade the account to Pay As You Go (recommended)"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "In the Oracle console, open <0>Billing & Cost Management</0>, then <1>Upgrade and Manage Payment</1>, and choose Pay As You Go. Your card is already on file from sign-up. Always Free resources stay free after the upgrade, and Oracle's own terms exempt Pay As You Go accounts from idle reclamation. Everything this installer creates is within the free allowance, so the upgrade does not by itself cost anything."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "What changes is that the card can now be charged if something outside the free tier is ever created. The installer closes that off from both ends: it refuses a server size above the free allowance, and it writes an Oracle quota policy that stops the account creating anything outside the Always Free compute shapes at all, whether or not you have upgraded. That policy is a real limit rather than a warning: Oracle refuses the request instead of billing you for it. On top of that it sets a spending alert that emails you the moment the account is billed one unit of your currency. Nothing needs adding to the command in Step 2."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Option 2: stay on the trial and donate spare time to Folding@home"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "<0>Folding@home</0> is a research project that simulates how proteins fold, to help understand diseases such as Alzheimer's, cancer and, yes, diabetes. With this option the installer adds its client to the server and runs it from 02:00 to 04:00 UTC each night. Two hours a day of real computation is well above Oracle's idle threshold, so the server is never a candidate for reclamation, and the time goes to something useful."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "The client runs at the lowest priority the system has, so Nocturne and its alarms always come first. To choose this option, add <0/> to the command in Step 2:"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Work is contributed anonymously by default. To count it towards a team, add <0/> as well. Trial accounts cannot be charged, but the installer still creates the spending alert in case the account is upgraded later."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Keep the run alive if Cloud Shell disconnects"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Cloud Shell closes idle sessions, which stops the installer part-way. Run <0/> first and the run survives a dropped connection. Detach with Ctrl-B then D, and come back to it with <1/>."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Sets up a spending alert that emails you if the account is ever charged, and a quota policy that stops the account creating anything outside the Always Free compute shapes"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Creates a virtual network with ports 80 and 443 open, in your home region"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Reserves a public IP address so it never changes"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Generates an SSH key in your Cloud Shell home if you do not have one"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Starts an Ampere A1 server with 1 core and 6 GB of memory, half the Always Free allowance, retrying when Oracle has no free capacity"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Installs Folding@home on a nightly schedule if you asked for it above"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "On the server: opens the firewall, installs Docker, downloads the Nocturne release bundle, generates the database passwords and starts everything once DNS resolves, checking each of these again on every run"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "What the script does"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "If Oracle has no free capacity"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Ampere A1 servers are popular and regions often run out. The installer tries every availability domain in your region and keeps retrying for 30 minutes. It already asks for the smallest size the installer offers, 1 core and 6 GB, so there is nothing smaller to fall back to. If it gives up, run the command again later: it remembers your domain, so the command no longer needs it, and everything already created is reused. Upgrading to Pay As You Go also tends to end these errors, since paying accounts are served first when servers are scarce."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "To have it keep trying for longer instead of re-running by hand:"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "If anything goes wrong, run it again"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Running the installer a second time is the fix for almost any failure. It looks every resource up before creating it, and checks the server itself on every run, so a run that was interrupted or stopped part-way carries on from where it got to rather than starting over. It remembers your domain, so the command on its own is enough."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "The script ends by printing your <0>instance key</0>. It is the master credential for this installation, used for administrative API access and account recovery. Save it in a password manager. Running the script again shows it again."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Download the SSH key the script created. It lives only in your Cloud Shell home, which Oracle deletes after six months without a Cloud Shell session. Use the Cloud Shell menu, <0>Download</0>, and enter <1/>. Nocturne keeps running without it, but it is your only way onto the server."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Updates are automatic. Watchtower checks for new Nocturne images daily."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Nocturne starts again by itself when the server reboots."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "To get a shell on the server, run the SSH command the script printed. Everything lives in <0/>."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Nothing backs up your data automatically. In the Oracle console, under Block Storage, you can take a backup of the server's boot volume at any time. The free tier includes five volume backups, so take them by hand and delete old ones rather than attaching a scheduled policy."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "To remove everything, terminate the instance in the Oracle console, then delete the reserved public IP and the <0>nocturne</0> virtual cloud network."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Run Nocturne on Oracle Cloud's Always Free tier. One command in the browser sets up the server, the network, HTTPS and Nocturne itself. No SSH or Docker knowledge needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "What you need"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "No domain yet? Get one from deSEC"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "<0>deSEC</0> is a non-profit DNS provider that gives out free names under <1>dedyn.io</1>. With one of those, the installer creates the DNS records for you and there is nothing to configure by hand."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "The same works for a domain you already own if its DNS is hosted at deSEC. If it is hosted elsewhere, you add two records by hand in Step 3."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Before you start: keeping it free"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Oracle switches off Always Free servers on trial accounts that it judges idle for seven days in a row. Its measure is whether the processor, network and memory each stayed below 20 percent of capacity for nearly all of that week. A Nocturne server for one person does very little work by that yardstick, so left alone it is likely to be stopped after its first quiet week. There are two ways to prevent that, and one of them changes the command in Step 2, so pick now rather than later."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Pay As You Go suits most people: it is a single change in the Oracle console, adds nothing to the server, and paying accounts are served first when Ampere capacity is scarce. The trade-off is that the card already on file <0>can</0> be charged if anything outside the free allowance is ever created. Choose Folding@home instead if you would rather the account stay a trial that cannot be billed at all, and you do not mind a second piece of software with network access on the server that holds your health data."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Step 1: Open Cloud Shell"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Sign in to the <0>Oracle Cloud console</0> and click the terminal icon in the top-right toolbar, labelled <1>Developer tools</1>, then <2>Cloud Shell</2>. A terminal opens at the bottom of the page. It is already signed in as you, so there is nothing to install or configure."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Step 2: Run the installer"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Paste this into Cloud Shell, replacing <0/> with your domain. Nocturne will answer on that name and every site you create gets a subdomain under it, so pick something you are happy to keep. If you chose Folding@home above, add <1/> in front."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Early on it asks for a deSEC token. <0>Nothing appears on screen as you paste it</0> — the token is hidden, and the installer tells you how many characters it read so you can see the paste landed. If deSEC does not accept it you can paste another. Press Enter with nothing pasted and it prints two records for you to create by hand instead. Either way it keeps working while the server boots, and waits for the records before it requests certificates."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Step 3: Add the DNS records"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Skip this step if you gave the installer a deSEC token. Otherwise, at your domain provider, create two <0>A</0> records pointing at the IP address the script printed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "The second one is a wildcard. It makes every site's subdomain resolve without a record each. If you use Cloudflare, leave the proxy off for both records, shown as a grey cloud. Cloudflare's proxy cannot carry the certificates Nocturne issues for its own subdomains."
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Step 4: Create your first site"
msgstr ""

#: ../portal/src/routes/docs/installation/oracle-cloud/+page.svelte
msgid "Once the script reports that Nocturne is answering, open <0/> in a browser. You are taken to the setup page to name your first site and register a passkey, which is your phone's or computer's built-in unlock. Register a second device as soon as you can, because there is no password to fall back on."
msgstr ""

